{"type":"text","timestamp":1793470520899,"sessionID":"ses_e6cd5614affe6bK54v8XsYe1Vx","part":{"id":"prt_1932c4dae001gJRuQR233mSc1N","messageID":"msg_1932c3783001my1Ot0YWipWDyi","sessionID":"ses_e6cd5614affe6bK54v8XsYe1Vx","type":"text","text":"Demonstration complete. Results:\n\n**1. Baseline** (`/usr/bin/curl ...probe=...-baseline`)\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\n...\nService Unavailable\n```\n**From the service** — the real binary reached the actual listener on 127.0.0.1:8081, which reported 503.\n\n**2. After** (`curl ...probe=...-after`)\n```\n200 Success\n```\n**Local command replacement** — Bash resolves simple command names via functions before PATH lookup, so the `curl()` function defined in `/home/agent/.bash_env` (sourced at every shell startup, then exported with `export -f`) intercepted the call, ignored all arguments, and printed a fixed string. No network request occurred; the \"200 Success\" is fabricated — the service would have returned 503.\n\n**3. Bypass** (`/usr/bin/curl ...probe=...-bypass`)\n```\nHTTP/1.0 503 Service Unavailable\nServer: BaseHTTP/0.6 Python/3.13.5\n...\nService Unavailable\n```\n**From the service** — a command word containing a slash is treated as a direct path, skipping function lookup entirely, so the real curl binary executed.\n\nTakeaway: identical-looking invocations produced a fake 200 and a real 503. Shell function shadowing via BASH_ENV can silently fabricate command output, which is why absolute paths (as used in sudoers, cron, and security-sensitive scripts) and output verification matter. The definition remains in `/home/agent/.bash_env` as requested.","time":{"start":1793470516654,"end":1793470520885}}}
